    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "An approximate analytical solution for the infinite sinusoidal interface between two elastic half-spaces has been applied to study non-planar faults. We show how this solution is based on assumptions that ultimately limit its utility. We use a two-dimensional displacement discontinuity method (DDM) for quasi-static boundary value problems to investigate sinusoidal faults in an otherwise homogeneous and isotropic elastic material. The DDM incorporates a complementarity algorithm to enforce appropriate contact boundary conditions along the model fault. Graphical results for a range of fault geometries reveal the stick, slip, and opening distributions of wavy faults. As the number of sinusoidal waves or the amplitude to wavelength ratio is increased, mean slip decreases. Additionally, the fault geometry causes the slip to deviate from the elliptical distribution of a planar fault. We demonstrate that the mechanical behavior of wavy faults cannot be prescribed a priori. This necessitates implementation of the complementarity algorithm and precludes an analytical solution. We employ the terms lee and stoss instead of releasing and restraining bends because a local minima in slip may occur along lee sides, as well as stoss sides. In some cases, lee sides stick while stoss sides slip. General trends in the slip perturbation can be explained by the angular relationship between the local fault trace and the orientation of the remote principal stresses; however, the behavior along a wavy model fault cannot be explained by this relationship alone.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
